<Docs slug="adjust">
  <p>
    The panel on the right has four pages, picked from the icons on its
    edge &mdash; <a href="/docs/presets.html">Presets</a>, Adjustments,
    Crop &amp; Transform and <a href="/docs/masks.html">Masks &amp;
    Cleanup</a> &mdash; and most of the work happens under
    <strong>Adjustments</strong>. Each section's heading has an arrow
    that folds it away. Two
    buttons sit at the top: <strong>Auto Adjust</strong> and
    <strong>B&amp;W</strong>, which turns the photo black and white or back
    to color. The sections below them run in this order:
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>White Balance</strong> &mdash; Temp and Tint, or click the
      eyedropper and then click a neutral-gray pixel in the photo to balance
      from it. Fastest way out of a yellow room.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tone</strong> &mdash; Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows,
      Whites and Blacks. This is where most photos are won or lost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Presence</strong> &mdash; Texture, Clarity, Dehaze, Vibrance
      and Saturation. Texture and Clarity add bite to fine and medium detail;
      Dehaze cuts through haze, or adds it at negative values. Vibrance
      leaves skin alone where it can, so try it before Saturation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Curve</strong> &mdash; a point curve over a faint histogram.
      Pick the RGB curve or one color channel from the four buttons. Click
      or drag to add a point, and double-click a point or drag it off the
      graph to remove it. The <strong>Point Curve</strong> menu has Linear,
      Medium Contrast and Strong Contrast to start from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Color Mixer</strong> &mdash; Hue, Saturation and Luminance for
      eight color bands, Red through Magenta. Pick one of the three from the
      buttons under the heading to see its eight sliders. On a black and
      white photo the mixer becomes the <strong>B&amp;W Mix</strong>: the
      same eight bands, each setting how light that color turns out in
      gray.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Color Grading</strong> &mdash; four color wheels for Shadows,
      Midtones, Highlights and Global. Drag inside a wheel to tint that
      range, and double-click it to clear the tint. Click a wheel to show its
      sliders, plus <strong>Blending</strong> and <strong>Balance</strong>
      for how the ranges meet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Detail</strong> &mdash; Sharpening with Radius, Detail and
      Masking, then Denoise. Raise Masking to keep sharpening off smooth
      areas such as skin and sky.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Optics</strong> &mdash; <strong>Remove Chromatic
      Aberration</strong> lines up the red and blue fringes along
      high-contrast edges.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vignette</strong> &mdash; Amount, Midpoint, Roundness and
      Feather. The vignette follows the crop.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Grain</strong> &mdash; Amount, Size and Roughness, for a film
      look.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    With several photos selected, each slider becomes a row of step
    buttons. They move every selected photo up or down from its own value,
    and the middle button resets that slider on all of them. The curve and
    the color wheels work on one photo at a time.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Crop &amp; Transform</strong> straightens, crops and turns the
    photo in 90&deg; steps; the crop is kept when you move to another page.
  </p>

  <h2>Three habits that make it faster</h2>
  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Let the histogram start.</strong> <strong>Auto Adjust</strong>
      sets the tone sliders from the photo's own histogram, so you begin
      close and fine-tune from there. Click it at the top of the panel, or
      on a whole selection from the bar under the grid &mdash; each photo is
      read from its own histogram. <strong>Reset</strong>, beside the
      Adjustments heading, puts the sliders back to zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do one, then copy it.</strong> Photos from one shoot share the
      same light, so one good edit is usually most of the way for the rest.
      <strong>Copy Adjustment</strong> from a finished photo and tick the
      sections to carry, such as Tone and Color Grading but not Crop. Then
      select the rest of the shoot and <strong>Apply Adjustment</strong>.
      LightPhotos remembers which sections you ticked. One good frame
      becomes the look for the shoot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Save a look you reuse.</strong> When an edit is worth keeping
      across shoots, press <kbd>Cmd+Shift+N</kbd> to save it as a
      <a href="/docs/presets.html">preset</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <kbd>Cmd+Z</kbd> undoes the last change: a slider drag, a reset, a crop,
    a preset, Auto Adjust or a pasted adjustment. A change made to a whole
    selection undoes in one step. Ratings, flags, labels and rotation are not
    undone.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of this touches the original file. Every slider is an instruction
    stored in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder beside your photo, so you
    can close the app mid-edit, come back next week, or reset the whole photo
    without any harm done. To change only part of the photo, add a
    <a href="/docs/masks.html">mask</a>. When it looks right, the last step
    is <a href="/docs/export.html">export</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
